    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Substance use disorders are highly prevalent psychiatric conditions that have a high co-morbidity with anxiety disorders, but their precise neurobiological underpinnings are not fully understood. The bed nucleus of the stria terminalis (BNST) is a brain region within the extended amygdala network that is a major regulator of affective behavior and has been implicated in neural changes associated with drug-seeking behavior and pathological anxiety. With highly diverse neuromodulator and receptor expression, the functions of BNST neuronal subpopulations are thought to be highly dependent on their spatial locations, anatomical projections, and molecular identities. The oval nucleus of the BNST (ovBNST) has been widely studied for its role in anxiety-like behavior, but recent evidence points toward underappreciated heterogeneity in ovBNST function and molecular identity. For example, the ovBNST includes partially overlapping neuronal subgroups marked by Drd1, Sst, Pkcd, Ntsr1, and other components of behaviorally relevant neuromodulatory systems. An additional understudied ovBNST marker is the vasoactive intestinal peptide receptor 2 (Vipr2), and little is known about actions of endogenous Vipr2 ligands in the BNST, including the neuropeptides VIP and pituitary adenylyl cyclase activating polypeptide (PACAP). Immunohistochemical studies showed immunoreactivity of VIP-expressing axonal fibers densely innervating the mouse ovBNST. Anterograde viral tracing experiments in transgenic Vipr2-Cre mice revealed Vipr2-BNST outputs to brain areas relevant for affective behaviors, including subregions of the hypothalamus, thalamus, and midbrain. Chemogenetic neuromodulation during binge alcohol drinking experiments showed that activation of Vipr2-BNST neurons increased food intake and decreased ethanol preference. These results are the first to directly examine the connectivity and functionality of Vipr2 neurons in the BNST in binge alcohol drinking. Further studies will be needed to elucidate the possible roles of Vipr2-BNST neurons in other behaviors relevant to neuropsychiatric disorders.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
